# XSH sigaltstack()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.001
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       With an alternate stack set by sigaltstack() (returning 0) and
           SIGUSR1 caught with SA_ONSTACK, the handler's local variables lie
           within the stack's range.
test       t/001-runs-on-alt.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.002
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A first sigaltstack(&a, &o) reports SS_DISABLE in o; a second
           sigaltstack(&b, &o) reports a's ss_sp and ss_size with ss_flags
           not SS_DISABLE.
note       disputed (Q-20): ss_flags "may contain" SS_ONSTACK or
           SS_DISABLE, so reporting either is not plainly required; a
           FAIL is logged as INSPECT. Setup makes sure no alternate
           stack is set at start-up (some runtimes install one); the
           test is UNRESOLVED if one is.
test       t/002-oss.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.003
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       After an alternate stack is set and then disabled with
           SS_DISABLE, a SIGUSR1 caught with SA_ONSTACK runs its handler
           on the normal stack.
test       t/003-disable.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.004
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#ERRORS/ENOMEM
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       sigaltstack() with ss_size MINSIGSTKSZ - 1 fails with ENOMEM.
test       t/004-enomem.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.005
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2008
says       sigaltstack() with ss_flags SS_ONSTACK fails with EINVAL.
note       Issue 7 required EINVAL for any flag but SS_DISABLE; Issue 8
           (Defect 1187) narrowed that to SS_ONSTACK or invalid flags,
           so SS_ONSTACK is rejected in both. Linux accepts it as if it
           were 0 (docs/linux-deviations.md O.13).
test       t/005-einval-onstack.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.006
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       sigaltstack() with ss_flags 0x4000 (neither SS_DISABLE nor
           SS_ONSTACK) fails with EINVAL.
note       Setup makes sure 0x4000 is not SS_ONSTACK or SS_DISABLE.
           Since Issue 8 an implementation may define flags of its own;
           one that uses 0x4000 for such a flag would FAIL here, and the
           result needs a look.
test       t/006-einval-flags.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.007
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#ERRORS/EPERM
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       Inside a handler running on the alternate stack, sigaltstack() with a
           new stack fails with EPERM.
test       t/007-eperm.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.008
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A process with an alternate stack that execs itself finds, in the new
           image, sigaltstack(NULL, &o) reporting SS_DISABLE.
nomutant   the new image's state is set by exec in the kernel
note       disputed (Q-20): ss_flags "may contain" SS_ONSTACK or
           SS_DISABLE, so reporting either is not plainly required; a
           FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
test       t/008-exec-none.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.009
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
when       XSI
since      2001
says       In a handler running on the alternate stack, sigaltstack(NULL, &o)
           reports SS_ONSTACK in o.ss_flags.
note       The page says ss_flags may contain SS_ONSTACK or SS_DISABLE,
           which leaves reporting them unclear (standard question Q-20);
           a FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
test       t/009-reports-onstack.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.010
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A child with an alternate stack of MINSIGSTKSZ bytes and a trivial
           SIGUSR1 handler declared SA_ONSTACK runs the handler and exits
           normally.
note       MINSIGSTKSZ is the minimum stack size for a signal handler.
           On x86-64 with AVX-512 the kernel's signal frame is larger
           than glibc's and musl's MINSIGSTKSZ of 2048 (docs/linux-
           deviations.md O.16).
nomutant   the signal frame is built by the kernel
test       t/010-minsigstksz.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.011
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
when       XSI
since      2001
says       sigaltstack() with ss_flags SS_DISABLE, ss_sp NULL and ss_size 0
           succeeds and disables the stack.
note       The DESCRIPTION says the size is ignored with SS_DISABLE, but
           the ENOMEM condition has no exception for it (standard issue
           SI-47); a FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
test       t/011-disable-size-ignored.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.012
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2008
says       After the main thread sets an alternate stack, a new thread's
           SIGUSR1 handler (SA_ONSTACK, raised by that thread) does not
           run on the main thread's stack.
test       t/012-per-thread.c

assertion  XSH.sigaltstack.013
anchor     functions/sigaltstack.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
when       XSI
since      2008
says       With an alternate stack set in the main thread, a new
           thread's sigaltstack(NULL, &o) reports SS_DISABLE; after
           sigaltstack() with SS_DISABLE, sigaltstack(NULL, &o) reports
           SS_DISABLE.
note       disputed (Q-20): ss_flags "may contain" SS_ONSTACK or
           SS_DISABLE, so reporting either is not plainly required; a
           FAIL is logged as INSPECT. The behaviour of 003 and 012 stays
           required there.
test       t/013-reports-disabled.c
